<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mashhad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mashhad is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and an important Shia pilgrimage centre around the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Reza. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> made it a principal seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>. After Nader's assassination in 1747, his grandson Shahrukh held a reduced Afsharid realm centred on Mashhad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mashhad.jpg" alt="Mashhad" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALI0513 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_View_of_Koohsangi_street,_Mashhad,_Iran.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mashhad lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, about 740 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and about 250 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. It stands in the valley of the Kashafrud River, between the Binalood and Hezar Masjed mountain ranges, with hot summers, cold winters and about 250 millimetres of precipitation a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 9th century Mashhad was a small village known as Sanabad, lying with Tus on the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. It outgrew the surrounding villages and took its present name, meaning place of martyrdom, from the shrine of the eighth Shia Imam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-al-rida">Ali al-Rida</a>, who is buried there; the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> is buried in the same shrine. In 1389 Miran Shah, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, stormed Tus after a siege of several months and massacred 10,000 of its inhabitants; those who escaped settled near the shrine, Tus was abandoned, and Mashhad became the capital of the district.</p><p>In the 16th century the town suffered from the raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, who took it in 1507 and again entered it in 1544, plundering and killing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> did not retake it until 1598. He is said to have walked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> to Mashhad, and in the Safavid period the city gained further religious recognition. Between 1736 and 1796 it was the capital of Afsharid Iran, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, whose tomb is in the city. In 1796 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mashhad is the capital of Razavi Khorasan province. The Imam Reza shrine draws millions of pilgrims each year, and several theological schools are associated with it. The shrine's administration, the Astan Quds Razavi, owns much of the city's real estate. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, was born in nearby Tus, and many institutions in Mashhad bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mashhad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shahrokh Shah, Nader's grandson, blinded in 1750, survived as a nominal ruler in <b>Mashhad</b> until 1796, when Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar took the city and had Shahrokh tortured to reveal the remaining Afsharid treasures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmad then turned west to <b>Mashhad</b>, whose ruler Shahrukh Afshar acknowledged Afghan suzerainty, and sent an army north of the Hindu Kush that brought the Tajik, Hazara, Uzbek, Turkmen and other peoples of northern Afghanistan under his control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He was formally crowned shah in 1796 and in the same year took <b>Mashhad</b> from the blind Afsharid ruler Shahrokh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His campaigns established Zand control over Fars, with Shiraz as his capital from the mid-1760s, and over most of the Iranian heartland, including Isfahan, Azerbaijan and Gilan, though Khorasan remained under Nader Shah's blind grandson Shahrokh at <b>Mashhad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sunni and Shia Muslims share the core of their faith but differ in their understanding of leadership, in some laws and rituals, and in their sacred places: for Shia Muslims, the shrines of the imams at Najaf, Karbala and <b>Mashhad</b> are centres of pilgrimage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
